-- Formal semantics of lambda-box (λ□): the weak call-by-value evaluation
-- relation, in Arend.
--
-- `LambdaBox.ard` defines the SYNTAX only. This module adds the meaning of that
-- syntax, i.e. the object every future correctness statement about this
-- compiler is stated against ("the generated Java computes what λ□ says it
-- computes"). It is the source-language counterpart of `Formal/JavaEval.ard`,
-- and like it, nothing in the compiler imports it: a specification, not a pass.
-- Check it by naming it (`arend arend.yaml Formal.LambdaBoxEval`).
--
-- Sources / inspiration:
--   * MetaRocq erasure/theories/EWcbvEval.v -- `eval`, the canonical big-step
--     weak cbv relation for λ□ (this file is a transcription of it).
--       https://metarocq.github.io/html/MetaRocq.Erasure.EWcbvEval.html
--       https://github.com/MetaRocq/metarocq/blob/coq-8.20/erasure/theories/EWcbvEval.v
--   * MetaRocq erasure/theories/ECSubst.v   -- `csubst`, `substl`
--   * MetaRocq erasure/theories/EGlobalEnv.v -- `lookup_env`, `lookup_constructor`,
--     `iota_red`, `fix_subst`, `cunfold_fix`
--
-- ONE DELIBERATE EXTENSION, the axiom oracle (`LBSpec` below), which upstream
-- does NOT have. Every level of the reference pipeline was checked first:
--   * MetaRocq `EWcbvEval.v` has no axiom rule at all. `eval_prim` (:275)
--     evaluates primitive VALUES (int/float/string/array), never a primitive
--     OPERATION, and `has_axioms` (:1695) is a wellformedness flag only -- an
--     axiom is admitted into the environment and then cannot be evaluated.
--   * CertiRocq `LambdaBoxMut/wcbvEval.v` is explicit about it: axioms are a
--     "hack coding of axioms in environment" (:312) and evaluation RAISES on
--     one. So being stuck on an axiom is upstream λ□'s actual behaviour, and
--     was this file's until the oracle below.
--   * CertiRocq `LambdaANF/eval.v` is where the pipeline solves it, one IR
--     later, and is what `LBSpec` copies: `prims := M.t (list val -> option
--     val)` (:24) as a section variable `Variable (pr : prims)` (:28), used by
--     the relation's `BStep_prim` rule (:1331) with the premises in the order
--     "collect the arguments, look the primitive up, let it compute".
--   * peregrine `theories/EvalBox.v` -- the same oracle in the same shape on the
--     functional side (`Variable (pr : prims)` :25, the three-step lookup
--     :50-53), with `bstep_f_equiv` (:122) relating function to relation. That
--     is this file plus `Formal/LambdaBoxRun.ard` plus
--     `Formal/LambdaBoxSound.ard`, so the triple below is theirs, not invented
--     here.
--   * CompCert `Events.v` `external_call` -- the reason to QUANTIFY over the
--     oracle rather than define it, already cited by `RtSpec` in
--     `Formal/JavaValue.ard`. Both semantics take their runtime as a parameter,
--     so an agreement between them is an agreement modulo one shared
--     assumption, stated rather than hidden.
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHICH λ□ SEMANTICS? (the `WcbvFlags` choice, made once and baked in)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Upstream, `eval` is parameterized by
--   `Class WcbvFlags := { with_prop_case ; with_guarded_fix ; with_constructor_as_block }`
-- and each flag switches whole rules on and off. This file does NOT carry the
-- flags as a record parameter: all three are FIXED, because for each of them our
-- setting is already determined by the AST we consume and by the backend we
-- intend to prove correct. Instantiating instead of parameterizing removes the
-- rules that could never fire (and the `flag = false` premise on every rule that
-- could). The choices, with their justification:
--
--   * `with_constructor_as_block := true`. `LambdaBox.ard`'s `construct` carries
--     an `Array LBTerm` of ALL constructor fields, which is what Peregrine's
--     `ast box` pass produces and what `ToJava.ard` compiles (one `Rt.Data`
--     allocation per `construct` node). So `eval_iota_block` / `eval_proj_block`
--     / `eval_construct_block` are the rules that apply, and their higher-order
--     counterparts (`eval_iota`, `eval_proj`, `eval_construct`, and the
--     `tConstruct ind c []`-is-an-atom case of `atom`) are dropped.
--     NOTE the asymmetry with `Serialize.ard`: EXPORT un-blocks a `construct`
--     into curried `tApp`s because Peregrine's wellformedness checker runs with
--     `cstr_as_blocks = false`. That is a property of the serialized form, not
--     of the terms we evaluate and compile.
--
--   * `with_prop_case := false`. The propositional rules (`eval_iota_sing`,
--     `eval_proj_prop`) fire on inductives with `ind_propositional = true`,
--     which erasure is supposed to have removed already -- see
--     `OneInductiveBody.indPropositional` in `LambdaBox.ard` ("Should always be
--     false"). This is MetaRocq's own `opt_wcbv_flags`/`target_wcbv_flags`
--     setting. The `= false` premise survives as an explicit side condition of
--     `evalIota`/`evalProj` (via `constructorInfo`), so a propositional
--     inductive does not evaluate rather than evaluating differently.
--
--   * `with_guarded_fix := false` (MetaRocq's `target_wcbv_flags`). This is the
--     one genuine choice, and it is dictated by the backend. `ToJava.ard`
--     compiles `fix` to a local class with one method per definition, and a
--     sibling is reached as a forwarding closure whose `apply` CALLS that
--     method: so the body runs as soon as the closure is applied, with no
--     inspection of any argument. (The justification is the same one the
--     earlier mutable-`Rt.Fn[]`-knot encoding had, and it survived that
--     encoding being replaced -- what matters is only that application
--     triggers the unfolding.) That is `eval_fix'` (unguarded), not
--     `eval_fix`/`eval_fix_value` (guarded).
--     Consequently `FixDef.principalArgIdx` is read (it is the `narg` of
--     `cunfold_fix`) but never constrains a reduction here, exactly as upstream
--     under those flags. The guarded rules are also the ones that would force a
--     `mkApps` spine analysis into every `app` rule.
--
-- Rules dropped because the corresponding SYNTAX is absent from `LBTerm`
-- (see the header of `LambdaBox.ard`): `eval_cofix_case`, `eval_cofix_proj`
-- (no `tCoFix`), `eval_force` (no `tLazy`/`tForce`), and the `tEvar` case.
--
-- Two node kinds have NO rule at all, deliberately:
--   * `bvar` -- a de Bruijn index under no binder is not a value and does not
--     reduce; upstream `tRel` is likewise absent from `atom` and from every
--     rule. Evaluation of a closed term never reaches one.
--   * `fvar` -- a locally-nameless free variable, our addition to λ□; it is the
--     analogue of `tVar`, which upstream is also stuck (`Serialize.ard` prints
--     it as `tVar_UNREACHABLE`, `ToJava.ard` compiles it to a throwing call).
-- So `Eval spec env (bvar n) v` and `Eval spec env (fvar x) v` are EMPTY types,
-- which is the statement "this term does not evaluate".
--
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SHAPE OF THE ARENDIFICATION
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Arend has no Coq-style indexed inductive families: a `\data`'s constructors
-- cannot instantiate its parameters freely. The idiomatic replacement is to
-- ELIMINATE the parameter that is determined syntactically -- here the term
-- being evaluated -- so `Eval` is declared `\elim t`, one clause per `LBTerm`
-- constructor. This costs nothing (every upstream rule is already keyed by the
-- shape of its subject) and buys inversion for free: `\case` on an `Eval`
-- automatically knows which rules could have produced it. The RESULT `v` stays a
-- parameter, and the rules that determine it (the atoms, and the two congruence
-- rules) say so with an explicit `v = ...` argument, the way arend-lib writes
-- relations.
--
-- Everything else follows `ToJava.ard`'s termination discipline: functions that
-- recurse into an `Array LBTerm` child do it INLINE via `xs i` inside
-- `mkArray`, never through a separate generic helper (see `csubst`).

\import LambdaBox
\import Arith.Nat   -- the `DecSet Nat` instance behind `==`
-- Only `mkArray`: importing Data.Array wholesale would shadow String's `++`.
\import Data.Array (mkArray)
\import Data.Bool
\import Data.Maybe
\import Data.String
\import Set
\import StringUtil (nthArr)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Nat and Array helpers.
--
-- The few standard-library operations the transcription needs that are not
-- borrowed: `Nat.compare` and `List.skipn`/`List.rev`. `List.nth_error` IS
-- borrowed -- `StringUtil.nthArr`, shared with the Java fragment's semantics --
-- and equality is `Set.==` throughout. arend-lib's `drop` is deliberately not
-- used: its result type carries `ndrop n l.len`, which would put length
-- arithmetic into `iotaRed` and into every rule that mentions it.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Coq's `Nat.compare`, used by `csubst` exactly as upstream uses it.
\data Comparison | cLt | cEq | cGt

\func natCompare (k n : Nat) : Comparison \elim k, n
  | 0, 0 => cEq
  | 0, suc _ => cLt
  | suc _, 0 => cGt
  | suc k, suc n => natCompare k n

\func natPred (n : Nat) : Nat \elim n
  | 0 => 0
  | suc n => n

-- `List.skipn`.
\func dropArr {A : \Type} (n : Nat) (xs : Array A) : Array A \elim n, xs
  | 0, xs => xs
  | suc _, nil => nil
  | suc n, _ :: xs => dropArr n xs

\func revAppendArr {A : \Type} (xs acc : Array A) : Array A \elim xs
  | nil => acc
  | x :: xs => revAppendArr xs (x :: acc)

-- `List.rev`.
\func revArr {A : \Type} (xs : Array A) : Array A => revAppendArr xs nil


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Substitution (ECSubst.v).
--
-- `csubst t k u` replaces de Bruijn index `k` by `t` inside `u`. This is the
-- CLOSED substitution: it never lifts `t`, because in a weak cbv semantics
-- everything substituted is a closed value. That is what makes it cheap enough
-- to be the only substitution λ□ needs (upstream's general `lift`/`subst` from
-- ELiftSubst.v are only used to relate `csubst` to PCUIC, which we do not do).
-- Indices above `k` are decremented (`natPred`), i.e. the binder disappears.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func csubst (t : LBTerm) (k : Nat) (u : LBTerm) : LBTerm \elim u
  | box => box
  | bvar n => \case natCompare k n \with {
    | cEq => t
    | cGt => bvar n
    | cLt => bvar (natPred n)
  }
  | fvar x => fvar x
  | lambda na m => lambda na (csubst t (suc k) m)
  | letIn na b b' => letIn na (csubst t k b) (csubst t (suc k) b')
  | app u v => app (csubst t k u) (csubst t k v)
  | const kn => const kn
  | construct ind c args =>
    construct ind c (mkArray {LBTerm} {args.len} (\lam i => csubst t k (args i)))
  | case ind npars discr brs =>
    case ind npars (csubst t k discr)
      (mkArray {\Sigma (Array BinderName) LBTerm} {brs.len}
        (\lam i => ((brs i).1, csubst t ((brs i).1.len Nat.+ k) (brs i).2)))
  | proj p discr => proj p (csubst t k discr)
  | fix defs idx =>
    fix (mkArray {FixDef LBTerm} {defs.len}
          (\lam j => \new FixDef LBTerm (defs j).defName
                                        (csubst t (defs.len Nat.+ k) (defs j).defBody)
                                        (defs j).principalArgIdx))
        idx
  | prim pv => prim pv

-- `substl defs body = fold_left (fun bod t => csubst t 0 bod) defs body`:
-- substitute the whole array, LEFTMOST FIRST, each time for index 0. So the
-- head of `defs` becomes index 0 of `body`.
\func substl (defs : Array LBTerm) (body : LBTerm) : LBTerm \elim defs
  | nil => body
  | d :: ds => substl ds (csubst d 0 body)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The global environment (EGlobalEnv.v).
--
-- Decidable equality of `Kername` is needed by `lookup_env`; `Kername` is a
-- `ModPath` plus an `Ident`, and `ModPath` is recursive, so it gets its own
-- Bool-valued equality. `Ident = String` uses `Set.==`; `Nat` uses `natEq`
-- (spelled out rather than pulling in `Arith.Nat`'s instance).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func dirPathEq (a b : DirPath) : Bool \elim a, b
  | nil, nil => true
  | nil, _ :: _ => false
  | _ :: _, nil => false
  | x :: a, y :: b => (x == y) and dirPathEq a b

\func modPathEq (a b : ModPath) : Bool \elim a, b
  | mpFile p, mpFile q => dirPathEq p q
  | mpFile _, mpBound _ _ _ => false
  | mpFile _, mpDot _ _ => false
  | mpBound _ _ _, mpFile _ => false
  | mpBound p x m, mpBound q y n => dirPathEq p q and (x == y) and (m == n)
  | mpBound _ _ _, mpDot _ _ => false
  | mpDot _ _, mpFile _ => false
  | mpDot _ _, mpBound _ _ _ => false
  | mpDot p x, mpDot q y => modPathEq p q and (x == y)

\func kernameEq (a b : Kername) : Bool =>
  modPathEq a.kerModPath b.kerModPath and (a.kerName == b.kerName)

-- `lookup_env`: scan from the HEAD, which is the declaration added LAST (see the
-- ORDER note on `GlobalDeclarations` in `LambdaBox.ard`).
\func lookupEnv (env : GlobalDeclarations) (kn : Kername) : Maybe GlobalDecl \elim env
  | nil => nothing
  | e :: env => \case kernameEq e.1 kn \with {
    | true => just e.2
    | false => lookupEnv env kn
  }

\func lookupConstant (env : GlobalDeclarations) (kn : Kername) : Maybe ConstantBody =>
  \case lookupEnv env kn \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just (constantDecl cb) => just cb
    | just (inductiveDecl _) => nothing
  }

\func lookupInductive (env : GlobalDeclarations) (ind : InductiveId)
  : Maybe (\Sigma MutualInductiveBody OneInductiveBody) =>
  \case lookupEnv env ind.indMutualBlock \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just (constantDecl _) => nothing
    | just (inductiveDecl mib) => \case nthArr ind.indIdx mib.indBodies \with {
      | nothing => nothing
      | just oib => just (mib, oib)
    }
  }

\func lookupConstructor (env : GlobalDeclarations) (ind : InductiveId) (c : Nat)
  : Maybe (\Sigma MutualInductiveBody OneInductiveBody ConstructorBody) =>
  \case lookupInductive env ind \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just (mib, oib) => \case nthArr c oib.indCtors \with {
      | nothing => nothing
      | just cb => just (mib, oib, cb)
    }
  }

-- `constructor_isprop_pars_decl`, with the constructor body replaced by the one
-- number the rules read from it (`cstr_nargs`): is the inductive propositional,
-- how many parameters does the block have, how many fields does constructor `c`
-- have.
\func constructorInfo (env : GlobalDeclarations) (ind : InductiveId) (c : Nat)
  : Maybe (\Sigma Bool Nat Nat) =>
  \case lookupConstructor env ind c \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just (mib, oib, cb) => just (oib.indPropositional, mib.indNpars, cb.cstrNargs)
  }

-- `cstr_arity mdecl cdecl = ind_npars + cstr_nargs`: in constructor-block form
-- a `construct` node carries the parameters too, so this is the length its
-- argument array must have.
\func constructorArity (env : GlobalDeclarations) (ind : InductiveId) (c : Nat) : Maybe Nat =>
  \case lookupConstructor env ind c \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just (mib, _, cb) => just (mib.indNpars Nat.+ cb.cstrNargs)
  }


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Reduction helpers (EGlobalEnv.v).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- `iota_red npar args br = substl (rev (skipn npar args)) br.2`: drop the
-- inductive parameters, then substitute the remaining fields for the branch's
-- binders. The `rev` is what makes the LAST binder de Bruijn index 0 -- the
-- convention `ToJava.ard`'s `pushFields` implements by pushing left to right.
\func iotaRed (npars : Nat) (args : Array LBTerm) (br : \Sigma (Array BinderName) LBTerm) : LBTerm =>
  substl (revArr (dropArr npars args)) br.2

-- `fix_subst mfix = [tFix mfix (n-1), ..., tFix mfix 0]` (n = #|mfix|), i.e. the
-- knot: every mutually defined function, most recent index first, so that inside
-- a body de Bruijn 0 is the LAST definition of the block. `ToJava.ard`'s
-- `pushFix` mirrors this ordering with its `Rt.Fn[]` slots.
\func fixSubstAux (defs : Array (FixDef LBTerm)) (n : Nat) : Array LBTerm \elim n
  | 0 => nil
  | suc n => fix defs n :: fixSubstAux defs n

\func fixSubst (defs : Array (FixDef LBTerm)) : Array LBTerm => fixSubstAux defs defs.len

-- `cunfold_fix`: the body of definition `idx` with the whole block substituted
-- for its recursive references, together with that definition's principal
-- argument index (`rarg`). Under unguarded fix the `narg` is only carried, never
-- compared -- see the flag discussion in the header.
-- (`nthArr`'s element type is given explicitly: left to inference it is solved
-- at the level variables of THIS definition, and the `defBody` read off the
-- result then lands in a copy of `LBTerm` that is not the one `substl` expects.)
\func cunfoldFix (defs : Array (FixDef LBTerm)) (idx : Nat) : Maybe (\Sigma Nat LBTerm) =>
  \case nthArr {FixDef LBTerm} idx defs \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just d => just (d.principalArgIdx, substl (fixSubst defs) d.defBody)
  }


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- THE AXIOM ORACLE.
--
-- An axiom is a constant with no body (`cstBody = nothing`): `Nat.add`,
-- `prim_add_int`, everything `JavaAxioms.ard`'s `javaAxioms` table realizes.
-- λ□ says nothing about one, so before this the relation was STUCK on every
-- program that did arithmetic -- while the backend compiled it to a `Rt.java`
-- constant and the Java semantics happily computed. That asymmetry is what
-- `LBSpec` removes, in the shape CertiRocq's `LambdaANF/eval.v` uses (see the
-- header): a map from name to a partial function on values, taken as a
-- parameter of the semantics rather than defined by it.
--
-- TWO FIELDS, NOT ONE, mirroring `RtSpec` (`Formal/JavaValue.ard`) field for
-- field. CertiRocq needs only `prims` because LambdaANF's `Eprim x f ys e`
-- carries all arguments at once; λ□ applies an axiom CURRIED, one `app` node per
-- argument, exactly as generated Java applies `Rt.Fn`. So both sides need to
-- know when enough arguments have arrived, and an arity written down is the fix
-- for the `Rt.curry` class of bug (`RtSpec.builtinArity` says the same).
--
-- Anything an instance leaves `nothing` is stuck: a named gap, not a wrong
-- answer. `lbNone` below leaves everything `nothing` and so recovers the
-- upstream transcription exactly.
--
-- WHAT AN INSTANCE OWES, in CompCert's `external_call` style -- properties
-- assumed of the oracle, not proved here:
--   * `axiomRun` returns a VALUE (`Eval spec env v v` for what it returns).
--     `LBTerm` is terms and values in one type, so this cannot be stated as a
--     signature the way CertiRocq's `list val -> option val` does.
--   * it is a function, hence deterministic, which is what keeps `Eval`
--     deterministic.
-- Neither is enforced; an instance that violates them makes the relation say
-- something false, the same way a wrong `Rt.java` would.
\class LBSpec
  -- How many arguments the axiom collects before it computes. A name with no
  -- arity is not an axiom this oracle knows, and stays stuck.
  | axiomArity : Kername -> Maybe Nat
  -- What it computes, given exactly that many argument VALUES, left to right.
  | axiomRun : Kername -> Array LBTerm -> Maybe LBTerm

-- No oracle: every axiom stuck, i.e. λ□ as MetaRocq and CertiRocq have it.
-- `Formal/JavaEvalRuns.ard`'s `rtNone` is the same instance on the Java side.
\func lbNone : LBSpec \cowith
  | axiomArity _ => nothing
  | axiomRun _ _ => nothing

-- Walk an application spine to its head, gathering the arguments.
--
-- `acc` is built by PREPENDING while walking inward (right to left), which
-- leaves the arguments in left-to-right order without a reverse or a snoc:
-- `app (app (const kn) a1) a2` gives `(kn, a1 :: a2 :: nil)`.
--
-- `nothing` unless the head is a `const` -- that is the only head an axiom can
-- have, and it is what makes this cheap to test on every application.
\func axiomSpine (t : LBTerm) (acc : Array LBTerm) : Maybe (\Sigma Kername (Array LBTerm))
  \elim t
  | const kn => just (kn, acc)
  | app f a => axiomSpine f (a :: acc)
  | _ => nothing

-- "This application is a saturated call to an axiom the oracle knows": the head
-- is a `const` with a declared arity, and the spine carries exactly that many
-- arguments.
--
-- Tested on the WHOLE application (`app fv av`), not on the function part, so
-- the spine walk collects the final argument itself -- there is nothing to
-- append, and the length test is exact, mirroring `applyVal`'s `got'.len == k`
-- in `Formal/JavaEval.ard` one for one.
--
-- UNDER-application is `nothing`, and then `evalAppCong` makes the partial
-- application a value -- `app (const kn) a1` for a binary axiom, the λ□ twin of
-- the Java side's `vBuiltin nm (a1 :: nil)`. No rule is needed for it: the
-- congruence rule already covers it, since `appCongOk (const _)` is `true`.
-- OVER-application is `nothing` too, and stays a stuck value, which is again
-- what the Java side does (a `vBuiltin` whose collected arguments outgrow its
-- arity never fires).
--
-- AN ARITY-0 AXIOM NEVER COMPUTES, because this is only ever consulted on an
-- `app`. Deliberate, and the same gap the Java side has: `rtField` gives back
-- `vBuiltin nm nil`, which also only fires when applied.
\func axiomSat (spec : LBSpec) (t : LBTerm) : Maybe (\Sigma Kername (Array LBTerm)) =>
  \case axiomSpine t nil \with {
    | nothing => nothing
    | just (kn, args) => \case axiomArity {spec} kn \with {
      | nothing => nothing
      | just arity => \case args.len == arity \with {
        | false => nothing
        | true => just (kn, args)
      }
    }
  }


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The side condition of the application congruence rule.
--
-- Upstream (`eval_app_cong`), an application whose function part evaluated to
-- `f'` is a VALUE only when `f'` cannot start a redex:
--   ~~ (isLambda f' || isFix f' || isBox f' || isConstructApp f' || isPrimApp f')
-- (with `isFix` rather than `isFixApp` because our fix is unguarded, and without
-- `isLazyApp` because there is no `tLazy`). Note the asymmetry, which is
-- reproduced exactly: `isLambda`/`isFix`/`isBox` look at `f'` ITSELF, whereas
-- `isConstructApp`/`isPrimApp` look at the HEAD OF ITS APPLICATION SPINE.
-- Hence two functions: `appCongOk` for `f'`, and `appCongOkSpine` for the head
-- of an `app` chain.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func appCongOkSpine (g : LBTerm) : Bool \elim g
  | app g' _ => appCongOkSpine g'
  | construct _ _ _ => false
  | prim _ => false
  | box => true
  | bvar _ => true
  | fvar _ => true
  | lambda _ _ => true
  | letIn _ _ _ => true
  | const _ => true
  | case _ _ _ _ => true
  | proj _ _ => true
  | fix _ _ => true

\func appCongOk (f : LBTerm) : Bool \elim f
  | box => false
  | lambda _ _ => false
  | fix _ _ => false
  | construct _ _ _ => false
  | prim _ => false
  | app g _ => appCongOkSpine g
  | bvar _ => true
  | fvar _ => true
  | letIn _ _ _ => true
  | const _ => true
  | case _ _ _ _ => true
  | proj _ _ => true


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The evaluation relation (EWcbvEval.v, `eval`).
--
-- `Eval env t v` : "in the global environment `env`, the closed term `t`
-- evaluates to the value `v`" -- big-step, weak (no reduction under binders),
-- call-by-value, deterministic-by-construction in the sense that at most one
-- rule applies to a given `t` and premise set.
--
-- One clause per `LBTerm` constructor; the rule names are the upstream ones.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\data Eval (spec : LBSpec) (env : GlobalDeclarations) (t v : LBTerm) \elim t
  -- `atom`: box, abstractions and (unguarded) fixpoints are already values.
  | box => evalAtomBox (v = box)
  | lambda na b => evalAtomLambda (v = lambda na b)
  | fix defs idx => evalAtomFix (v = fix defs idx)

  -- `eval_prim` / `evalPrimInt`: a primitive integer evaluates to itself.
  -- (Upstream's `primArray` case, which evaluates the payload, has no
  -- counterpart: `PrimTag` has only `primInt` -- see `LambdaBox.ard`.)
  | prim pv => evalPrim (v = prim pv)

  | const kn => {
    -- `eval_delta`: unfold a constant to its body.
    | evalDelta {cb : ConstantBody} {body : LBTerm}
        (lookupConstant env kn = just cb)
        (cb.cstBody = just body)
        (Eval spec env body v)

    -- AN AXIOM IS A VALUE, if the oracle declares an arity for it. No upstream
    -- counterpart: this is the extension the header names.
    --
    -- The premises are the three things that make it one, in the order the
    -- runner checks them: the constant is DECLARED, it has NO body (which is
    -- what "axiom" means, and what makes this rule disjoint from `evalDelta` --
    -- so the relation stays deterministic), and the oracle knows it. A declared
    -- bodyless constant the oracle does not know stays stuck: the gap stays a
    -- gap rather than becoming a value.
    --
    -- Being a value is ALL this rule says; computing happens at the application
    -- that saturates it (`evalAxiomApp`). That split is what lets a curried
    -- axiom collect its arguments through the ordinary congruence rule, and it
    -- is the same split the Java side makes between `rtField` (the runtime
    -- constant is an `Fn`) and `builtinRun` (it computes when full).
    | evalAxiom {cb : ConstantBody} {arity : Nat}
        (lookupConstant env kn = just cb)
        (cb.cstBody = nothing)
        (axiomArity {spec} kn = just arity)
        (v = const kn)
  }

  -- `eval_zeta`: let-binding, call-by-value.
  | letIn _ b0 b1 => evalZeta {b0v : LBTerm}
      (Eval spec env b0 b0v)
      (Eval spec env (csubst b0v 0 b1) v)

  | app f a => {
    -- `eval_box`: applying a proof/erased value stays erased. The argument is
    -- still evaluated (to some `av`), so `app` is strict in both positions.
    | evalBox {av : LBTerm}
        (v = box)
        (Eval spec env f box)
        (Eval spec env a av)

    -- `eval_beta`
    | evalBeta {na : BinderName} {b av : LBTerm}
        (Eval spec env f (lambda na b))
        (Eval spec env a av)
        (Eval spec env (csubst av 0 b) v)

    -- `eval_fix'` (unguarded): the fixpoint unfolds on application, without
    -- inspecting the principal argument. `narg` is `cunfold_fix`'s first
    -- component, carried and unused -- see the flag discussion in the header.
    | evalFix {defs : Array (FixDef LBTerm)} {idx narg : Nat} {fn av : LBTerm}
        (Eval spec env f (fix defs idx))
        (cunfoldFix defs idx = just (narg, fn))
        (Eval spec env a av)
        (Eval spec env (app fn av) v)

    -- THE ORACLE FIRES: a saturated call to an axiom it knows.
    --
    -- `BStep_prim` (CertiRocq `LambdaANF/eval.v:1331`) with the premises in its
    -- order -- evaluate to get the arguments, look the primitive up, let it
    -- compute -- differing only in that λ□ collects the arguments through a
    -- curried spine, so `axiomSat` does the "look it up and check it is full"
    -- step in one place shared with the runner.
    --
    -- The spine is tested on `app fv av`, i.e. AFTER both parts have values, so
    -- `args` already ends with `av` and every argument in it is a value.
    --
    -- `kn` and `args` are EXPLICIT for the reason `evalArgsT-sound` in
    -- `Formal/LambdaBoxSound.ard` gives for its arrays: left implicit, they are
    -- solved out of `axiomSat`'s nested `\case`s, which the elaborator defers
    -- and then reports as an unsolved metavariable at the NEXT premise.
    | evalAxiomApp {fv av : LBTerm} (kn : Kername) (args : Array LBTerm)
        (Eval spec env f fv)
        (Eval spec env a av)
        (axiomSat spec (app fv av) = just (kn, args))
        (axiomRun {spec} kn args = just v)

    -- `eval_app_cong`: everything else that is a value applied to a value is a
    -- value (a stuck application: an axiom the oracle does not know applied to
    -- arguments, or one still collecting them). `eval_construct` -- the
    -- higher-order rule that lets a constructor accumulate arguments through
    -- `tApp` -- is NOT here: in constructor-block form a `construct` node is
    -- always saturated, and `appCongOk` rejects a construct-headed spine
    -- accordingly.
    --
    -- THE SECOND PREMISE IS THE ORACLE'S EXCLUSION, and it is what keeps the
    -- relation deterministic: without it, a saturated axiom call would both
    -- compute (`evalAxiomApp`) and be a stuck value (here). It belongs with
    -- `appCongOk`, whose whole meaning is "this cannot start a redex" -- with an
    -- oracle, a saturated axiom spine CAN. It is a premise rather than a
    -- conjunct inside `appCongOk` so that the runner and the soundness proof can
    -- hand the equation straight across; a `\case` on `axiomSat` inside
    -- `appCongOk` would not reduce under a hypothesis about it (the quirk
    -- `Formal/NodePathUnique.ard`'s header describes).
    -- It comes AFTER the argument's premise, so that `av` is already solved when
    -- it is checked -- upstream's three premises keep their order, and the new
    -- one is appended to them.
    | evalAppCong {f' av : LBTerm}
        (Eval spec env f f')
        (appCongOk f' = true)
        (Eval spec env a av)
        (axiomSat spec (app f' av) = nothing)
        (v = app f' av)
  }

  -- `eval_construct_block`: a constructor is saturated by construction, so it is
  -- a congruence -- evaluate every field, left to right, and rebuild the node.
  -- `vs` is indexed by `args.len`, which is how "same length, pointwise" is
  -- said in Arend without a separate `All2` relation.
  | construct ind c args => evalConstruct {arity : Nat} {vs : Array LBTerm args.len}
      (constructorArity env ind c = just arity)
      (args.len = arity)
      (\Pi (i : Fin args.len) -> Eval spec env (args i) (vs i))
      (v = construct ind c vs)

  -- `eval_iota_block`: evaluate the scrutinee to a constructor of THIS inductive
  -- (`ind` is shared with the pattern, which is upstream's way of stating it),
  -- select the branch by constructor index, drop the parameters and substitute
  -- the fields.
  | case ind npars discr brs => evalIota
      {c nargs : Nat} {args : Array LBTerm} {br : \Sigma (Array BinderName) LBTerm}
      (Eval spec env discr (construct ind c args))
      (constructorInfo env ind c = just (false, npars, nargs))
      (nthArr c brs = just br)
      (args.len = npars Nat.+ nargs)
      ((dropArr npars args).len = (br.1).len)
      (Eval spec env (iotaRed npars args br) v)

  -- `eval_proj_block`: primitive projection out of a single-constructor
  -- (constructor 0) block; the field index is offset by the parameters, the
  -- same `npars + idx` `ToJava.ard` computes.
  | proj p discr => evalProj {nargs : Nat} {args : Array LBTerm} {a : LBTerm}
      (Eval spec env discr (construct p.projIndType 0 args))
      (constructorInfo env p.projIndType 0 = just (false, p.projParamCount, nargs))
      (args.len = p.projParamCount Nat.+ nargs)
      (nthArr (p.projParamCount Nat.+ p.projFieldIdx) args = just a)
      (Eval spec env a v)


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Program evaluation.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- An `LBProgram` is its declarations plus a body; running it is evaluating the
-- body in those declarations. This is the left-hand side of every future
-- correctness statement ("... and the generated Java prints `v`").
\func EvalProgram (spec : LBSpec) (p : LBProgram) (v : LBTerm) : \Type =>
  Eval spec p.1 p.2 v


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Sanity checks: small closed programs, evaluated by exhibiting a derivation.
-- These are the tests of this module -- they typecheck only if the rules fit
-- together AND the helper functions (`csubst`, `fixSubst`, `substl`, `iotaRed`,
-- the environment lookups) compute the values the rules ask for, since every
-- side condition below is discharged by `idp`.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func idTerm : LBTerm => lambda anon (bvar 0)

-- None of these four mentions an axiom, so each holds for ANY oracle and takes
-- one as a parameter. That is not only more general: a parameter of class type
-- is a LOCAL INSTANCE in Arend, which is what lets the nested constructors below
-- find their `spec` (with `lbNone` written in instead, `evalIdFix`'s innermost
-- `evalAtomLambda` has nothing to resolve it from and fails to infer).

-- (\lam x => x) box ==> box
\func evalId (spec : LBSpec) : Eval spec nil (app idTerm box) box =>
  evalBeta (evalAtomLambda idp) (evalAtomBox idp) (evalAtomBox idp)

-- \let x := box \in x ==> box
\func evalLet (spec : LBSpec) : Eval spec nil (letIn anon box (bvar 0)) box =>
  evalZeta (evalAtomBox idp) (evalAtomBox idp)

-- A primitive integer is its own value.
\func evalPrimSelf (spec : LBSpec)
  : Eval spec nil (prim (primInt, 7)) (prim (primInt, 7)) => evalPrim idp

-- The one-definition knot: `fix { f := \lam x => x } 0` applied to `box`.
-- Exercises `fixSubst` (the knot array), `substl` and `csubst` at once:
-- unfolding replaces the recursive reference inside `\lam x => x` -- there is
-- none, but the index shifting still has to come out right -- and the result is
-- applied by beta.
\func idFixDefs : Array (FixDef LBTerm) => (\new FixDef LBTerm anon idTerm 0) :: nil

\func evalIdFix (spec : LBSpec) : Eval spec nil (app (fix idFixDefs 0) box) box =>
  evalFix (evalAtomFix idp) idp (evalAtomBox idp)
          (evalBeta (evalAtomLambda idp) (evalAtomBox idp) (evalAtomBox idp))


-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- And the two oracle rules, against an oracle small enough to derive by hand.
--
-- `Formal/LbAxioms.ard` is the real instance, and `Formal/Agreement.ard` runs it
-- on real programs; what these two check is that the rules themselves fit
-- together -- that `axiomSat`'s spine walk and length test agree with what
-- `evalAxiom`/`evalAxiomApp` ask for, every side condition again by `idp`.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

\func sndKn : Kername => \new Kername (mpFile nil) "snd"

-- One BINARY axiom, returning its second argument. Binary because that makes the
-- partial application real (the inner `app` has to be a value, which only
-- `evalAppCong` can make it), and "second argument" because it pins the ORDER
-- `axiomSpine` collects in -- an axiom computing something symmetric would not.
\func lbSnd : LBSpec \cowith
  | axiomArity kn => \case kernameEq kn sndKn \with { | true => just 2 | false => nothing }
  | axiomRun kn args => \case kernameEq kn sndKn, args \with {
    | true, _ :: y :: nil => just y
    | _, _ => nothing
  }

-- Declared and bodyless: what makes it an axiom rather than a missing name.
\func sndEnv : GlobalDeclarations =>
  (sndKn, constantDecl (\new ConstantBody nothing)) :: nil

-- Unapplied, the axiom is a value -- `evalAxiom`, the rule with no upstream
-- counterpart.
\func evalAxiomIsValue : Eval lbSnd sndEnv (const sndKn) (const sndKn) =>
  evalAxiom idp idp idp idp

-- Applied to ONE of its two arguments it is a stuck value, by the ordinary
-- congruence rule and with no new rule needed -- the λ□ twin of the Java side's
-- `vBuiltin nm (a1 :: nil)`.
\func evalAxiomPartial
  : Eval lbSnd sndEnv (app (const sndKn) (prim (primInt, 1)))
                      (app (const sndKn) (prim (primInt, 1))) =>
  evalAppCong evalAxiomIsValue idp (evalPrim idp) idp idp

-- Applied to both it saturates, so the oracle computes -- and the answer is the
-- SECOND argument, so the spine really is in left-to-right order.
\func evalAxiomFires
  : Eval lbSnd sndEnv (app (app (const sndKn) (prim (primInt, 1))) (prim (primInt, 2)))
                      (prim (primInt, 2)) =>
  evalAxiomApp sndKn (prim (primInt, 1) :: prim (primInt, 2) :: nil)
               evalAxiomPartial (evalPrim idp) idp idp
